    summary: "Nigella sativa seeds, known as black seed or black cumin, are used in cooking and in traditional medicine for inflammation, infections and cancer. Memorial Sloan Kettering's About Herbs summary cites small clinical trials for asthma, allergy, dyspepsia, diabetes and fatty liver, and anticancer effects of thymoquinone in the laboratory. In cancer care, oral seeds lowered the incidence of febrile neutropenia and shortened hospital stay in children with brain tumours, and a topical gel decreased the severity of acute radiation dermatitis in women with breast cancer; MSK says larger studies are needed. Contact dermatitis and a case of eosinophilia with systemic symptoms after topical oil are reported, and high doses may cause liver damage, with human data lacking. Black seed inhibits CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 in vitro and thymoquinone inhibited warfarin activity, both of unknown clinical significance.",
